WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.110
Film Courage: Dialogue is not conversation?

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.390
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: It’s conversation

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.200
with a purpose.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:15.790
It’s people conversing but every line needs
to move the story forward in some respect,

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:21.110
whether it’s moving the plot forward through
some sort of revelation that a character makes

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:27.890
or moves these characters forward in their
own relationship because they have an argument

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:33.370
and that conflict causes one or both of them
or more of them to shift in terms of their

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:41.879
thought processes and feelings or it provides
some exposition, some information, some data

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:47.299
which is a key to help them get a clue to
move the thing forward.

00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:54.079
But yes dialogue is conversation but it’s
conversation with a purpose.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:56.639
You need to be really mindful of that.

00:00:56.639 --> 00:01:00.780
So that’s tricky because it’s got to be
entertaining too.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:07.940
And in an ideal world it’s more often then
not it’s subtextual so that what is being

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:13.340
communicated is really more about what is
underneath the words than the words themselves.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.150
It’s also dialogue is also the words that
aren’t spoken.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.050
Sometimes the best dialogue is what is not
said.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:33.170
So I get the message, is dialogue conversation
and the subtext there is don’t make it just

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.270
like how people talk in real life like because
we just blabber on and on.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.120
We don’t have that amount of time in a screenplay.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.000
You need to be precise and efficient.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.659
But yes it is conversational.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:52.170
It should sound conversational like how characters
would talk in the real world.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.490
So I get it but it’s conversation with a
purpose.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:55.750
That would be my take on that.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:00.190
Film Courage: Are there any films that are
a great study in subtext?

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:12.400
Scott: I would say read anything by Tony Girl
like MICHAEL CLAYTON, that script.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:24.180
He’s just an amazing writer and MICHAEL
CLAYTON is a script that just anybody should

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:33.120
read because he not only has subtext going
on but the text is so entertaining and there

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.400
is layers to it so sometimes he’s saying
what is absolutely on the character’s mind

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.930
so the text is like right there and the meaning
of the words are right up top but then there

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.049
are other layers underneath too.

00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:51.110
So he is really brilliant and MICHAEL CLAYTON
is a script I absolutely recommend.

